  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason — whether outcomes are probable (i.e., probability > 0.5) — is not considered by the individual. In fact, both winning outcomes have probabilities (0.1% and 0.2%) below 0.5, so neither is classified as probable. Therefore, the average number of probable outcomes is zero for both lotteries, and the reason would predict indifference. However, the individual does not consider this threshold or the concept of probable outcomes at all. Instead, the justification is based solely on the higher potential gain in A, ignoring the probabilistic categorization. Thus, the individual did not apply the decision reason. The reasoning process is entirely different and based on expected value or gain magnitude, not on the probabilistic classification defined in the reason. There is no mention or use of the threshold (1/2), nor of any evaluation of probable outcomes. Therefore, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
